WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.438 --> 00:00:11.980
STEVE FRANCIA:
Welcome to my session,

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:16.140
"Enterprise-Ready Modern
Applications with Go--

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.790
The Rise of Digital Chaos."

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:21.070
For years, one of the
most talked-about topics

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:23.110
has been digital transformation.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:25.450
It seems like everyone's
feeling the need

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:29.020
to modernize their legacy
enterprise applications.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:32.350
As technology has specialized,
it has grown exponentially

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:34.220
in complexity.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:36.560
Two decades ago there might
have been a dozen choices

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.760
that needed to be made, with
typically two or three options.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:43.790
Today the amount of choices
is simply staggering.

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:45.950
Nobody can possibly
begin to understand

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:47.930
all that is required.

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:50.870
3/4 of CIOs say managing
performance is now

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:54.290
impossible due to
the IT complexity.

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:58.250
Security risks have seen a
similar exponential expansion.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:02.870
It seems like every day we hear
about another major breach.

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:05.780
The historical approach
to enterprise architecture

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.360
doesn't work in the digital age.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:10.430
If we don't dramatically
change our approach,

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:14.950
and soon, it will be too late.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:18.040
Today I'm here to share
with you what the path is.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.770
We need to modernize our
enterprise applications,

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:23.110
and a lot of folks
have this wrong.

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:25.000
They have part of the
picture, but haven't

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.760
grasped that true
modernization requires

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:33.230
simplifying our approach,
not simply containerizing it.

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:34.270
Who am I?

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:37.730
I've spent the last decade and
a half leading industry-changing

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:40.760
organizations, focused
on bringing simplicity

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:42.050
to the chaos.

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:44.240
As senior vice
president at MongoDB,

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.990
I led the development of the
first non-relational database

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:50.930
to enter mainstream
use in 45 years.

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:53.750
As chief operator at
Docker, I led the effort

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:55.700
to simplify containers.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.740
As a side project
I created Hugo,

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:01.080
the most secure and highest
performance web engine.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.510
Today I'm one of the leads
of the Go Team, where

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.475
we are building a modern,
simple Cloud-native programming

00:02:06.475 --> 00:02:06.975
language.

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:12.020
Since you might not
be familiar with Go,

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:15.350
I'll first provide a short
overview of what Go is.

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:17.420
Then I'll share with you
what a modern enterprise

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:19.250
application requires.

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.620
Lastly, I'll share with
you how Go and Google

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:25.750
Cloud will help you with
migration to modern enterprise.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:28.390
Along the way I'll share
quotes from enterprises,

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:31.870
and how Go helped them.

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:35.440
Go is a modern programming
language designed by Google

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:38.110
for Google's problems
of building and serving

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:39.715
server-side software at scale.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:46.100
Google uses Go extensively
for a wide range of things,

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.410
from our indexing platform
that powers Google

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:51.620
Search to the infrastructure
that Google Cloud is built on.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:58.260
Launched a decade ago, Go
has seen explosive growth.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.410
Today, Go is a top 10
language, with broad adoption

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:05.850
among the world's
leading enterprises.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:07.890
Go follows Einstein's Maxim--

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:11.040
be as simple as
possible, but no simpler.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.710
As Go is designed for
maximum simplicity,

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:16.080
it is very easy to
learn and to work with.

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:22.280
Go drives the balance between
productivity and performance,

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:25.400
sharing the friendliness of
dynamic languages like Python

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:30.080
and the productivity of compiled
languages like C and Java.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:32.210
As a modern language,
Go was designed

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:35.750
from the very beginning with
security and safety in mind.

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:38.990
Over the past 10 years, Go has
developed a large and mature

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:41.350
ecosystem.

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:44.560
Go is loved by both developers
and employers alike,

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.210
ranked as the
number one language

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:47.770
across many industry surveys.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.160
Go is widely loved by
developers because it

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:56.980
makes building modern
software simple and secure.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:00.790
But what does it take to build
modern enterprise applications?

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:02.865
First of all, they
must minimize risk.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:07.850
They must be fully modern.

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:11.780
Adding modern components onto
or alongside legacy systems

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:15.650
introduces significantly
higher risk and complexity.

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:19.399
Instead of simplifying things,
they multiply the complexity.

00:04:19.399 --> 00:04:22.880
You now need to worry about
many systems, languages,

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:23.840
and integration points.

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:27.900
They must be safe.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.730
Modern applications
have to be built

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:31.710
in a way that ensures safety.

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:34.020
According to researchers
at Microsoft,

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:37.830
70% of the security bugs that
the company fixes each year

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:40.020
relate to memory safety.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:43.320
Google found a similar 70%
of serious bugs in Chrome

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:46.050
are memory management
and safety bugs.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:48.180
Legacy programming
languages were not

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:50.740
designed with safety in
mind, because at the time

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:52.860
there was no such
thing as a cyberattack

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.870
and bits were at a premium.

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:57.630
Our environment today is vastly
different from the one legacy

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.700
languages were designed for.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:05.210
Modern languages massively
de-risk applications.

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:06.860
They must be secure.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:09.380
One of the largest
risks systems face

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:12.050
is in the form of lacking
security, which results

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:14.390
in leaks and attack vectors.

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:16.310
In the constant
battle for security,

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:18.530
applications need to
utilize the latest

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:20.390
encryption and protocols.

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:22.400
They need to run in
minimal environments

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:28.080
with maximum isolation and
minimal external dependencies.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.390
They need to not depend
on legacy libraries

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:31.260
and environments.

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:38.580
Modern enterprise applications
must adapt to business needs.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:40.770
One way they do
this is by utilizing

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:42.690
service-oriented APIs.

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:45.810
Approaching applications with
a communication-first focus

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:49.470
ensures that the application
has the proper separation,

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:52.920
scalability, and flexibility
to adapt to new business

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.220
requirements.

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:57.780
This approach also isolates each
feature for greater flexibility

00:05:57.780 --> 00:05:59.970
and security.

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:03.240
They must be architected to
be modular and composable.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:06.120
Tech debt can be a
technologist's worst enemy.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:09.490
This hidden tax slows
down all development.

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:11.950
Traditional inheritance-based
architectures

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:14.050
create code that is
very tightly coupled,

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:16.150
and hard to adapt
to new requirements

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:18.280
and very hard to maintain.

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:20.080
Which brings us to
our next point--

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:22.420
they must be easily
maintainable.

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:25.070
Teams and priorities
are constantly changing.

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:27.820
We need code that is easy
to understand and pick up

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:30.640
by a constant influx
of new engineers.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:33.190
We want applications that
require minimal maintenance

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:36.920
without constant
runtime upgrades.

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:39.320
Our final theme is that
modern enterprise apps

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:41.730
must sustain growth.

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:43.920
They must be scalable
and efficient.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:46.440
Data and usage has been
expanding exponentially

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:48.300
for the past five decades.

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.760
With the end of Moore's
Law and increased pressure

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:53.700
on computing costs, modern
enterprise applications

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.860
need rapid deployment
and the ability

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:59.820
to scale up and down with ease,
using resources efficiently

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:03.890
and keeping operating and
management costs down.

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:06.310
They must have a
Cloud-native architecture.

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:08.170
Modern enterprise
applications must

00:07:08.170 --> 00:07:10.960
take advantage of innovations
in Cloud computing.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:13.030
With the Cloud-native
architecture,

00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:15.580
they must be designed with
automation, monitoring,

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:18.340
and repair from the beginning,
favoring managed services.

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:23.500
They must incorporate
event-driven components,

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:25.480
also known as serverless.

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:28.150
Event-driven features
must be properly utilized

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:31.330
to bring cost savings,
adaptability, and efficiency

00:07:31.330 --> 00:07:33.700
to modern enterprise
applications.

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:35.830
This requires software
that loads instantly

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:37.750
with minimal
resource consumption

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:41.680
and minimal dependencies.

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:43.680
We're now going to go
through these requirements

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:46.140
and highlight the ways
that Go and Google Cloud

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:50.310
Platform will help you build
modern enterprise applications.

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:56.810
Go and GCP help
you minimize risk.

00:07:56.810 --> 00:08:00.230
Go is a truly modern language,
requiring a completely clean

00:08:00.230 --> 00:08:03.260
implementation, keeping
your application decoupled

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:05.280
from legacy libraries.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:06.770
While there is
risk in a rewrite,

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:08.870
it pales in comparison
to the larger risk

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:11.420
of sticking with legacy
systems, or worse,

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:14.870
maintaining both a legacy
and a modern system.

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:18.290
Go's high learnability,
productivity, and simplicity

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:20.600
minimize the rewrite
risk, and often pay

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:23.690
dividends in massively
reducing both operating

00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:25.970
costs and technical
debt, further

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:28.780
minimizing business risk.

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:31.780
Zviad, a Senior Principal
Engineer at Dropbox,

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:35.830
said, "People become very
productive in Go very fast.

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:38.169
Our infrastructure
is built in Go today,

00:08:38.169 --> 00:08:42.130
and all the new things
we build in Go."

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:44.110
Legacy software
languages were designed

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:48.160
for a different era where safety
wasn't prioritized, or worse,

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:50.830
often compromised
for performance.

00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:53.770
This trade-off made sense
in a pre-network era,

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:56.980
but has created massive
risk in today's environment.

00:08:56.980 --> 00:08:58.960
Modern languages
like Go are designed

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:02.590
for today's environment,
where safety is critical.

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:05.710
Go is memory safe,
type safe, and utilizes

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:08.140
low-latency garbage collection.

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:11.470
These features alone avoid
over 70% of critical bugs

00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:15.130
resulting from unsafe
legacy languages.

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:18.520
Bala, a Senior Director of
Engineering at PayPal, wrote,

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:21.040
"In our tightly managed
environments where we run Go

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:24.610
code, we have seen a CPU
reduction of around 10% versus

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:29.640
C++, with cleaner and
more maintainable code."

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:32.950
Go has been designed to create
the most secure applications,

00:09:32.950 --> 00:09:35.730
minimizing risk as
much as possible.

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.550
Go applications compile
down to a single binary

00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:40.860
without any local dependencies.

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:42.990
It's not uncommon to
see an application built

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:45.990
using only the standard
library, or only a couple

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:48.640
well-vetted Go dependencies.

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:51.370
Go's dependency management
uses an industry-leading,

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:54.550
tamper-evident transparency
log, with built

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:56.920
in tooling that ensures
that your dependencies are

00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:59.110
exactly what you expect.

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:01.040
Go has native
encryption, which is

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:02.820
used to power much
of the internet,

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:06.930
including key components
of Google and Cloudflare.

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:09.570
Adopting Go means that
the next Heartbleed, which

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:11.880
impacted virtually every
application written

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:15.800
in any other language, won't
impact your application.

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:18.500
Go even supports
distroless containers,

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:22.400
where there are zero local
dependencies to worry about.

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:26.300
GCP, CI/CD, and Artifact
Manager have direct access

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:29.240
to Go's Vulnerability
Database and can provide you

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:31.370
instant warnings about
security threats.

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:39.610
Finally, Go and GCP will help
you adapt to business needs.

00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:42.560
Service-oriented APIs
provide enhanced agility,

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:46.270
scalability, and reliability,
allowing your business

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:49.630
to adapt to market and
strategy changes quickly.

00:10:49.630 --> 00:10:52.390
Go is the only language
explicitly designed

00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:55.360
to build service-oriented APIs.

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:57.580
Go has native support
in the standard library

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:00.730
for all you need to build
performant and secure APIs,

00:11:00.730 --> 00:11:03.220
including a
high-performance web server.

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:04.870
This is Go's most
common use case,

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:10.540
with over 70% of Go developers
using Go for building APIs.

00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:12.850
Matt, a Principal Software
Engineer at Monzo,

00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:15.910
shared, "Go is a perfect
language for creating

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:17.950
microservice architectures.

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:20.830
The concurrency features,
and the language in general,

00:11:20.830 --> 00:11:24.430
has allowed the easy creation
of small and simple network

00:11:24.430 --> 00:11:27.440
services at Monzo."

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:30.860
A unique feature of Go is
that its application structure

00:11:30.860 --> 00:11:32.750
directly mirrors
the architecture

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:35.180
of service-oriented systems.

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:37.610
Services are designed
independently

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:41.570
in a share-nothing architecture
communicating across networks.

00:11:41.570 --> 00:11:45.560
Go applications are composed
of share-nothing modules

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:50.510
communicating across channels,
a native feature of Go.

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:53.240
This means that both your
service and your application

00:11:53.240 --> 00:11:55.760
composed of many
services are using

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:58.280
the same architectural pattern.

00:11:58.280 --> 00:12:00.530
The benefits of
agility, independence,

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:02.900
and easy maintenance
are twice as impactful

00:12:02.900 --> 00:12:06.110
for Go-based services,
maximizing your ability

00:12:06.110 --> 00:12:08.240
to adapt to business needs.

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:10.190
There's an additional
cognitive benefit

00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:13.670
for developers of using a
single architectural pattern

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:17.490
at every level of your system.

00:12:17.490 --> 00:12:20.640
John Graham-Cumming,
the CTO at Cloudflare,

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:24.090
shared about Go, "I came
for the easy concurrency,

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:28.080
I stayed for the
easy composition."

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:31.650
Kubernetes creator, Joe Beda,
tweeted, "Kubernetes success

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:34.770
is in part due to Go
and the Go community.

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:40.900
Go hits the sweet spot for
distributed system software."

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:43.210
High maintenance costs
ensure that your technology

00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:46.510
can't properly adapt or
support business needs.

00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:49.540
Go is unique among all languages
as it has been designed

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:51.820
to minimize maintenance costs.

00:12:51.820 --> 00:12:54.070
Rapidly-evolving teams
are common at Google

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:56.350
and virtually all enterprises.

00:12:56.350 --> 00:13:00.010
Go's simplicity, easy
readability, and single-binary

00:13:00.010 --> 00:13:02.500
deployment ensures that
Go applications have

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:04.030
minimal maintenance
and that it's

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:06.640
relatively easy for
someone new to a code base

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:09.860
to safely maintain it.

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:11.990
Martin, a software
engineer at Trivago,

00:13:11.990 --> 00:13:14.480
spoke of this hidden
benefit of Go.

00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:17.570
"Go's simplicity and its
sophisticated tooling

00:13:17.570 --> 00:13:20.940
let us scale not only our
service, but more importantly,

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:23.900
the process of software
engineering itself.

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:27.080
Reducing the friction of
onboarding and training someone

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:30.650
has a significant impact on
the company's productivity,

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:32.480
even more so at a
constantly moving

00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:35.080
environment like Trivago."

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:37.510
And Benjamin Cane, Vice
President and Principal

00:13:37.510 --> 00:13:41.380
Engineer at American Express,
shared, "After working on Go,

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:42.970
most of our
developers don't want

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:44.500
to go back to other languages."

00:13:47.900 --> 00:13:53.300
Go and GCP will help you
sustain business growth.

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:55.910
Historically, CTOs have
needed to make an impossible

00:13:55.910 --> 00:13:59.330
trade-off-- either choose to
delay launch as the team builds

00:13:59.330 --> 00:14:03.530
a scalable application first, or
choose to ship a slow prototype

00:14:03.530 --> 00:14:06.770
early, acknowledging that
development will be delayed

00:14:06.770 --> 00:14:11.500
significantly later as adoption
growth demands a rewrite--

00:14:11.500 --> 00:14:13.330
effectively having
to choose when

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:16.062
to be unable to support
business growth.

00:14:16.062 --> 00:14:18.360
Go has eliminated this dilemma.

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:21.480
Go is designed for Google Scale
with built-in concurrency that

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:25.080
takes full advantage of today's
multicore machines and Cloud

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:26.520
Services.

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:28.620
Go enables programmers
to quickly develop

00:14:28.620 --> 00:14:33.730
applications that perform
at scale, no rewrite needed.

00:14:33.730 --> 00:14:36.670
Eric, a Software Engineering
Manager at MercadoLibre,

00:14:36.670 --> 00:14:41.740
wrote, "With Go, the company
obviated 88% of their servers

00:14:41.740 --> 00:14:44.410
and cut CPU on the
remaining ones in half,

00:14:44.410 --> 00:14:48.240
producing a tremendous
cost savings."

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:50.760
Cloud computing brings
countless benefits

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:54.150
for flexibility and efficiency,
critical to sustaining

00:14:54.150 --> 00:14:55.770
business growth.

00:14:55.770 --> 00:14:58.230
Go is the only modern
language that has first class

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:00.810
support on all Cloud platforms.

00:15:00.810 --> 00:15:03.690
Go is designed for
Cloud-native applications.

00:15:03.690 --> 00:15:06.360
Over 3/4 of the projects
in the Cloud-Native

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:09.350
Computing Foundation
are written in Go.

00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:11.660
And Go is designed for
container deployment,

00:15:11.660 --> 00:15:14.120
with a very small
footprint, compared

00:15:14.120 --> 00:15:18.170
to the "ship the world" legacy
languages like Java, Python,

00:15:18.170 --> 00:15:20.090
and Note.

00:15:20.090 --> 00:15:23.060
Mariano, a Software
Engineer at Movio,

00:15:23.060 --> 00:15:26.300
blogged, "Our Go
microservices currently build

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:30.170
in 5 seconds or less, they test
in one or two seconds which

00:15:30.170 --> 00:15:32.770
includes integration
tests, and they

00:15:32.770 --> 00:15:35.620
can deploy (via
Kubernetes) new containers

00:15:35.620 --> 00:15:37.750
in 10 seconds or less.

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:42.520
The key factor here
is small images."

00:15:42.520 --> 00:15:45.010
Among all Cloud
benefits, serverless

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:48.430
has the largest potential for
sustaining business growth,

00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:51.190
with its low cost,
instant scalability,

00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:53.410
and expanded flexibility.

00:15:53.410 --> 00:15:55.180
Go is an ideal fit
for serverless,

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:58.750
with broad support on
all serverless platforms.

00:15:58.750 --> 00:16:01.450
Go has very low
resource use and nearly

00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:04.120
instant startup times
compared to applications

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:08.110
written in legacy languages
that utilize interpreters.

00:16:08.110 --> 00:16:12.220
Google's Cloud Run is the ideal
platform for Go applications

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:15.660
with seamless deployment.

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:19.980
Go and GCP help you
simplify the chaos.

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:22.890
"The biggest impediment to
a company's future success

00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:26.320
is its past success,"
according to Dan Schulman,

00:16:26.320 --> 00:16:28.990
the CEO of PayPal.

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:32.500
Today's business environment is
defined by modern enterprises

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:36.580
with a "move fast and
break things" culture.

00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:39.070
Enterprises will need
to embrace the process

00:16:39.070 --> 00:16:41.770
of constant transformation
to remain relevant.

00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:46.610
Go provides the
flexibility and performance

00:16:46.610 --> 00:16:49.520
to assist organizations with
application modernization

00:16:49.520 --> 00:16:54.270
efforts, and GCP provides
flexible managed services

00:16:54.270 --> 00:16:57.120
to guide your organization
into the modern era.

00:17:00.910 --> 00:17:03.940
To learn more about Go,
its primary use case,

00:17:03.940 --> 00:17:05.980
and to read case
studies of enterprises

00:17:05.980 --> 00:17:10.270
who have transformed their
organizations, visit Go.dev.

00:17:10.270 --> 00:17:11.960
Thanks for spending
time with me today.

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:13.060
I hope that you've
learned something.

00:17:13.060 --> 00:17:14.435
I hope you enjoyed
it, and I hope

00:17:14.435 --> 00:17:16.960
to see you at a future
conference or one

00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:18.730
of the many Go conferences.

00:17:18.730 --> 00:17:20.580
Thank you.

